Cells, tissues, organs, and extracellular material (@med-tissues: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @medicine, #source @med-source-osu, #source @med-source-histology, #medicine @med-homeostasis) { r_55X7R8B6AC36G6R6XXPGBF0EQ9
**Read complete chapters:** [Anatomy & Physiology — Oregon State University](../sources/Anatomy-and-Physiology.lingua#r_1WEZWZ5YY6797K4EXVYTBJS25N), chapter 3 on cells and chapter 4 on tissues, including figures and review questions. Revisit [Chemistry for a living body — water, molecules, ions, and energy](Chemistry-and-fluids.lingua#r_3QGS68JVNCQ89FNB287Z9TXTAT) for a molecular term. For Portuguese parallel study, read the complete epithelial and connective tissue chapters in [Histologia — Tatiana Montanari](../sources/Histologia-Montanari.lingua#r_19MSRBKWFADMAH4P4TT7HG81KS), then its muscle and nervous tissue chapters as those structures are developed. Later returns to an individual tissue use smaller passages.

Cells maintain boundaries, exchange material, use energy, and respond to signals. Their organization and the material between them help determine what a tissue can do. Extracellular matrix/matriz extracelular includes structural proteins and a surrounding hydrated material; tissue is not simply a collection of identical cells.

Four broad tissue families recur throughout medicine: epithelium/epitélio covers and lines surfaces; connective tissue/tecido conjuntivo supports, connects, and transports; muscle/tecido muscular generates force; nervous tissue/tecido nervoso processes and transmits signals. An organ usually combines several families. A tendon contains living cells, matrix, vessels, and nerves; it is not an inert rope.

A microscope section is a prepared slice. Color, magnification, stain, and cutting direction influence what is visible. A dark band in one preparation cannot be interpreted like a dark band in an MRI.

**A bridge into microscopic images:** first locate cell membrane, nucleus, and extracellular space on a labeled illustration. Then distinguish an epithelial surface from a connective-tissue region beneath it. An organized collagen-rich tendon section and a cartilage section have different cells and matrix. This progression explains why a whole-joint image cannot show every microscopic process and why a microscopic change alone cannot describe the person's movement.

**Try:** draw a joint at three scales: the whole region, one tissue, and cells within that tissue. Identify what a gross anatomy image cannot show and what a microscope image cannot explain about whole-body movement.

**Continue:** [Homeostasis — regulation and changing demands](Homeostasis.lingua#r_1P1X68BT5R9BMY5DC8NQ0NZPVA).

